<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fulbert Youlou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fulbert Youlou was a Catholic priest and politician who led Middle Congo to autonomy in 1958 and became the first president of the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a> in 1960. His government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> grew authoritarian and corrupt, and he was forced to resign during the popular uprising of August 1963, known as the Trois Glorieuses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fulbert-youlou.jpg" alt="Fulbert Youlou" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Republic of the Congo, Ministry of Information · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fulbert_Youlou_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Youlou, whose last name means grape in Lari, was born in Madibou, a younger child in a family of three boys. He was baptised at nine, received the Christian name Fulbert, and entered the Petit Séminaire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> in 1929. He completed his studies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libreville">Libreville</a>, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaounde">Yaoundé</a> met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barthelemy-boganda">Barthélemy Boganda</a> and André-Marie Mbida, later nationalist leaders. He served as a priest in the parish of Saint-François de Brazzaville, directing youth organisations and Catholic groups.</p><p> In the territorial elections of 1947 he stood for the African college in the district of Pool and was heavily defeated. In 1955 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> council chose him as its candidate for the legislative elections, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> tried to dissuade him. He was banned from wearing the cassock and celebrating Mass. Some supporters regarded him as the reincarnation of Jesus-Matswa, and he became a symbol of resistance to colonial rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 17 May 1956 Youlou founded the Union démocratique de défense des intérêts africains, an anti-communist party of Liberal Christian persuasion whose symbol was the crocodile. In September 1957 it won a majority in the Assembly with 23 seats, and rivalry with Jacques Opangault's African Socialist Movement turned violent. In the referendum of 28 September 1958 he supported the Yes vote on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a>. On 28 November 1958 the socialist deputies left the Assembly, and Youlou proclaimed the Republic, was named head of the provisional government and became prime minister on 8 December.</p><p>In August 1960 he led the country into independence. He favoured economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>, pursued a pro-Western policy he called moderate and in December 1960 held a conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> that created the anti-communist groupe de Brazzaville. In August 1962 he announced a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>, and in August 1963 the unions refused his proposed system. Union leaders were imprisoned, and the revolution of the Trois Glorieuses followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> despised him, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> refused to assist him, and he resigned in the face of overwhelming opposition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Youlou was imprisoned after his resignation and on 25 March 1965 fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Léopoldville</a> with the help of his successor, Alphonse Massamba-Débat. A popular tribunal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Congo-Brazzaville</a> tried him in absence from 8 June 1965 on charges including misappropriation of public funds, and sentenced him to death. He answered with a book, J'accuse la Chine, in 1966. Later regimes held him responsible for the country's problems, and his name was struck from public discourse. He died of hepatitis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in 1972, and was buried in Madibou without official ceremony. The National Conference of 1991, which restored multi-party democracy, rehabilitated his memory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fulbert Youlou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Middle Congo became the autonomous Republic of the Congo in November 1958 under the priest-politician <b>Fulbert Youlou</b>, and gained full independence on 15 August 1960, keeping Brazzaville as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/middle-congo">Middle Congo</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Fulbert Youlou</b>, an anti-communist who had been the first black mayor elected in French Equatorial Africa, became the first president.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1952" aria-label="Madrid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4459 719 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Fulbert Youlou"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4909 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1952">What was here in 1952? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
